// What the Sunken Lantern's innkeeper has heard of the dungeon: one rumour
// for each thing a run may draw, and the line said once they are all told.
// `src/rumours.rs` reads it; townsfolk.ron's `rumours: true` says who tells
// them.
//
// Every field:
//   rumours: one row for each thing a run may draw, each (of: "...", say: "...")
//              of:   what it is of, by name: a floor ten boss or a mini-boss
//                    as monsters.ron names it, a biome as the log names
//                    it on arrival, or a stratum as `src/dungeon/strata.rs`
//                    names it; every boss and every mini-boss has one, and
//                    so has every stratum from the second to the fifth
//              say:  the words, said as written; always true of what is down
//                    there and never exact, so never which floor nor how to
//                    beat it; a floor ten boss's never names any floor ten
//                    boss, since which of them waits is kept until floor ten
//                    shows it
//   last:    said once every rumour of the run is told, and on every talk
//            after
//
// A run's rumours are told one a talk, in this order: the floor ten boss it
// drew, then each mini-boss it drew, shallowest first, then each biome it
// drew on a floor that could have drawn another, shallowest first and each
// once; floor ten's biome is its boss's, told by the boss's rumour; then the
// strata that have one, shallowest first, every run; a thing it did not draw
// is never told. Any of the lines may name a person by role,
// `{gatewarden}`, and says their name this run.
(
    rumours: [
        // The floor ten bosses.
        (of: "the Drowned King", say: "Nets come up with crowns in them some nights. Something down there still thinks it rules."),
        (of: "the Mother Below", say: "The well's tasted of eggs since spring. {gatewarden} says the lake has a mother."),
        (of: "the Ember Wyrm", say: "The lake steams over the deep water now, winter and all. Nothing cold breathes like that."),
        // The mini-bosses. The Bone Choir is the Choirmaster's, under his name.
        (of: "Grubnak the goblin king", say: "Goblins sing marching songs below, and they've got themselves a king."),
        (of: "the Widow", say: "A delver came back up wrapped in silk, and none of it his."),
        (of: "the Choirmaster", say: "There's singing near the crypts, and nobody down there has a throat left."),
        (of: "the Drowned Abbess", say: "The old abbey sank with its abbess at prayer. Some say she hasn't stopped."),
        (of: "the Forgemaster", say: "Somebody's working a forge under the lake. You can hear the hammer from the jetty."),
        // The biomes of floors one to nine, each told once a run draws it
        // from more than one, and each placed in its stratum: depth is time.
        // The Underlake has none while it stands alone on floors seven and
        // eight, since a run never draws it from more than one; the rumour of
        // its stratum, before the kingdom, tells of its lake instead.
        (of: "the Upper Halls", say: "Just past the gate it's the old king's halls. Broken stone and long corridors."),
        (of: "the Cellars", say: "Just past the gate it's cellars this season. Casks, damp, and rats the size of dogs."),
        (of: "the Watch Barracks", say: "The old watch kept its barracks just past the gate. Kobolds sleep in the bunks now."),
        // The Drowned Town's first wave.
        (of: "the Old Town", say: "Just past the gate there's streets, a whole town under ours. The lamps are lit and every door is open."),
        (of: "the Harbour", say: "Just past the gate it's the old harbour, boats still tied up. And a ship that never sailed."),
        (of: "the Sunken Library", say: "Past the town there's a library in the palace grounds, half full of water. Scholars went in. Pages came out."),
        (of: "the Drowned Cathedral", say: "The palace had a cathedral, and it's still standing in the water. Mind the bridges; the water's colder than it looks."),
        (of: "the Catacombs", say: "Beneath the kingdom you walk through the dead. They were buried. It didn't take."),
        (of: "the Old Mines", say: "Beneath the kingdom they dug for ore, and the props are older than I am. Mind what you lean on."),
        (of: "the Fungal Deeps", say: "Under the palace the rock opens into caves lit by mushrooms. Don't breathe what the big ones puff."),
        (of: "the Spider Warrens", say: "Under the palace it's webs, whole galleries of them. Burn what you can't cut."),
        (of: "the Forge of Embers", say: "Near the bottom the rock runs hot. Somebody forged there before the kingdom had a name."),
        (of: "the Ashen Galleries", say: "Near the bottom it's all ash and cinders, and imps that light themselves for fun."),
        // The Palace grounds' first wave.
        (of: "the Royal Wood", say: "The old king's hunting wood went down with the palace. The hounds are still running in it, and they've gone white."),
        (of: "the Palace Gardens", say: "The palace gardens are down there too, hedges and fountains and all. The king kept beasts in cages. The cages are empty now."),
        // The strata of the Undermere, told once each after the biomes, so a
        // run's hints of what lies deeper and older come in the order the
        // adventurer would walk them. The first and the last have none: the
        // gate says the first, and the floor ten boss's rumour the last.
        (of: "The Palace and its Grounds", say: "Past the town it's the palace, sunk whole, grounds and all. Kings walked there."),
        (of: "Beneath the Kingdom", say: "Below the palace is what the kingdom stood on. Mines, tombs, caves. The deeper you go, the older it gets."),
        (of: "Before the Kingdom", say: "Under the foundations nobody built a thing. The lake was there first, and so is what lives in it."),
        (of: "The Oldest Deep", say: "At the very bottom there was a people before the kingdom, and a fire they kept. The forges are theirs, not the king's."),
    ],
    last: "That's all I've heard. Come back when you've been somewhere.",
)
